1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,337
Welcome to Dot Goose, part one. Nine examples show how C sharp
becomes readable Goose code.

2
00:00:06,617 --> 00:00:11,545
We will compare the code, explain each result, and finish with
executable sizes.

3
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:13,260
First example.

4
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:15,297
A first translation.

5
00:00:15,847 --> 00:00:20,370
Explanation: This program prints Dot Goose, starts with forty,
and adds two.

6
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:25,557
The C sharp integer becomes I thirty two in Goose, and Write
Line becomes print.

7
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:30,716
Explanation: The condition still checks for forty-two before
printing the value.

8
00:00:30,996 --> 00:00:37,140
Both runs also print the wrapped result from the final
addition. We will unpack that in chapter four.

9
00:00:37,883 --> 00:00:38,886
Second example.

10
00:00:39,686 --> 00:00:40,753
Console escapes.

11
00:00:41,303 --> 00:00:44,631
Explanation: Look at the strings between the quotation marks.

12
00:00:44,911 --> 00:00:51,396
Only the call changes. The escape sequences for the tab,
quotes, backslash, and line breaks carry through.

13
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:58,512
Explanation: The first print writes an empty line. Later,
carriage return and newline remain distinct.

14
00:00:58,792 --> 00:01:04,061
The test compares raw output bytes, so even a change a
terminal might hide would fail.

15
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:05,717
Third example.

16
00:01:06,517 --> 00:01:07,712
Loops and branches.

17
00:01:08,262 --> 00:01:14,833
Explanation: The index starts at zero and stops before five.
The running total starts at negative two.

18
00:01:15,113 --> 00:01:20,275
At index two, the else branch changes the Boolean flag instead
of adding to the total.

19
00:01:21,008 --> 00:01:27,686
Explanation: The remaining additions take the total to six.
The final condition checks the total and the flag.

20
00:01:27,966 --> 00:01:32,126
Goose keeps the same loop and branch structure. Both runs
print six.

21
00:01:32,883 --> 00:01:33,801
Fourth example.

22
00:01:34,601 --> 00:01:35,689
Integer overflow.

23
00:01:36,239 --> 00:01:41,209
Explanation: This example adds one to the largest signed
thirty-two-bit integer.

24
00:01:41,489 --> 00:01:46,247
Unchecked C sharp arithmetic wraps around, giving the smallest
signed integer.

25
00:01:47,008 --> 00:01:54,198
Explanation: The Dot Goose helper adds the same bits as
unsigned values, then casts back to a signed integer.

26
00:01:54,478 --> 00:01:59,214
That preserves all three results even when Goose runs with
debug checks enabled.

27
00:01:59,967 --> 00:02:00,884
Fifth example.

28
00:02:01,684 --> 00:02:02,985
From classes to structs.

29
00:02:03,535 --> 00:02:11,066
Explanation: The scoped Counter class becomes a Goose struct.
The factory named new Counter takes the constructor argument.

30
00:02:11,346 --> 00:02:16,850
Add still looks like a method call. Its writable receiver lets
it update the counter in place.

31
00:02:17,592 --> 00:02:23,181
Explanation: Point is a value type. Copying it gives the
program an independent value.

32
00:02:23,461 --> 00:02:30,352
Changing the copy leaves the original sum at thirteen. The
copy prints five, after the counter prints forty-two.

33
00:02:31,092 --> 00:02:32,052
Sixth example.

34
00:02:32,852 --> 00:02:34,388
Defaults and struct copies.

35
00:02:34,938 --> 00:02:42,383
Explanation: The explicit Point constructor sets X and marks
the point ready. The parameterless construction uses defaults.

36
00:02:42,663 --> 00:02:46,311
That means zero for both coordinates and false for the ready
flag.

37
00:02:47,050 --> 00:02:54,901
Explanation: Changing the blank point's Y coordinate does not
set its flag. Its Value method still returns negative one.

38
00:02:55,181 --> 00:03:01,623
The assigned copy can change independently. The final values
are seven, negative one, and nine.

39
00:03:02,383 --> 00:03:03,429
Seventh example.

40
00:03:04,229 --> 00:03:05,786
Same name, different receiver.

41
00:03:06,336 --> 00:03:13,723
Explanation: Point and Counter each declare a method called
Read. The receiver type tells the compiler which method to

42
00:03:13,723 --> 00:03:13,973
use.

43
00:03:14,253 --> 00:03:18,499
Goose represents those methods as functions with distinct
receiver types.

44
00:03:19,258 --> 00:03:26,128
Explanation: Point reads seven. Counter reads twenty-one, and
Read Twice calls the counter version two times.

45
00:03:26,408 --> 00:03:33,576
Setting the enabled flag to false changes the counter to
negative one. Both runs produce the same sequence.

46
00:03:34,342 --> 00:03:35,259
Eighth example.

47
00:03:36,059 --> 00:03:37,936
Evaluation order with side effects.

48
00:03:38,486 --> 00:03:44,481
Explanation: The counter starts at ten. The left side of the
sum calls Next first, printing eleven.

49
00:03:44,761 --> 00:03:49,796
Then the two arguments to Take call Next again, printing
twelve and thirteen in that order.

50
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:56,246
Explanation: Take prints its first argument, twelve, and
returns its second argument, thirteen.

51
00:03:56,526 --> 00:03:59,939
The sum prints twenty-four, and the counter still holds
thirteen.

52
00:04:00,219 --> 00:04:06,406
Matching only the final value would miss a reordered print.
The test compares the entire sequence.

53
00:04:07,175 --> 00:04:08,156
Ninth example.

54
00:04:08,956 --> 00:04:10,620
Raw strings and interpolation.

55
00:04:11,170 --> 00:04:16,354
Explanation: Triple quotes let the JSON quotation marks stay
exactly as written.

56
00:04:16,634 --> 00:04:24,531
The compiler recognizes C sharp raw strings and keeps the same
form in Goose. A longer quote delimiter becomes an escaped

57
00:04:24,531 --> 00:04:25,445
Goose literal.

58
00:04:26,175 --> 00:04:34,487
Explanation: A dollar sign opens an interpolated C sharp
string. The expressions inside braces are parsed and bound

59
00:04:34,487 --> 00:04:35,860
like ordinary code.

60
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:44,796
Two dollar signs let single JSON braces stay literal. In
Goose, str joins the text and values, and the helper preserves

61
00:04:44,796 --> 00:04:46,615
C sharp Boolean spelling.

62
00:04:46,895 --> 00:04:51,631
The test also checks that expressions with side effects run in
the original order.

63
00:04:52,383 --> 00:04:58,847
Here are the measured file sizes for all nine programs. Each
row was checked against the dot net output.

64
00:04:59,127 --> 00:05:06,585
The first eight small Goose programs use about fourteen point
six kilobytes with system libraries. The raw string and

65
00:05:06,585 --> 00:05:09,581
interpolation example is shown in the last row.

66
00:05:09,861 --> 00:05:18,486
Compare the static Goose build with dot net Native A O T, and
the compressed self-contained dot net build. The small managed

67
00:05:18,486 --> 00:05:21,338
D L L needs an installed dot net runtime.

68
00:05:22,092 --> 00:05:27,148
The fourteen kilobyte Goose program still needs the system C
library and loader.

69
00:05:27,428 --> 00:05:35,526
The fully static build includes the C library. We verified
that all nine static programs ran with only their executable

70
00:05:35,526 --> 00:05:37,092
in an empty Linux root.

71
00:05:37,372 --> 00:05:45,213
That makes these console examples suitable for a Docker
scratch image on compatible Kubernetes nodes. A backend can

72
00:05:45,213 --> 00:05:48,486
still need resources such as T L S certificates.
